    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2.1, opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "“the hyperbolic equation together with special initial data”",
      "observation": "A Riemann problem comprises a hyperbolic equation and initial data, rather than initial data alone."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2.1, equation (1.11)",
      "anchor": "“piecewise constant with a single jump discontinuity at some point, say x = 0”",
      "observation": "The displayed initial condition is q(x,0) = q_l for x < 0 and q(x,0) = q_r for x > 0. The display has no branch at x = 0."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; equation (1.1) and its following paragraph",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + A q_x(x,t) = 0; q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝ^m",
      "observation": "The introductory one-dimensional constant-coefficient linear system uses a vector of m unknown functions and a constant real m × m matrix A. Subscripts denote partial derivatives."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "“real eigenvalues and a corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors”",
      "observation": "These are the stated conditions for hyperbolicity of the constant-coefficient system (1.1). Eigenvalues give the corresponding wave speeds."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; Section 1.1, equations (1.8)–(1.9)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0",
      "observation": "Conservation laws provide another governing-equation context. Here f is the flux function, and the quasilinear expression uses the flux Jacobian f′(q)."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; opening paragraphs preceding Section 1.2",
      "anchor": "“At a discontinuity in q, the partial differential equation (1.8) does not hold in the classical sense”",
      "observation": "The source distinguishes classical differential satisfaction from the integral conservation law, which continues to hold at discontinuities."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2.1, paragraph after (1.11)",
      "anchor": "“q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i”",
      "observation": "Neighboring cell averages can supply the two states; solving the resulting Riemann problem gives information for computing a numerical flux and updating cell averages over a time step."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2.1, paragraph after (1.11)",
      "anchor": "“typically a similarity solution, a function of x/t alone”",
      "observation": "The source describes typical hyperbolic Riemann solutions as depending on x/t and consisting of a finite set of waves propagating from the origin with constant wave speeds."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 30; printed page 8; Section 1.4, first two paragraphs",
      "anchor": "“a discontinuity in the medium at x = 0 as well as a discontinuity in the initial data”",
      "observation": "The source explicitly extends the Riemann-problem idea to discontinuous media and notes that variable-coefficient hyperbolic equations need not be in conservation form."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "x is the one-dimensional spatial coordinate and t is time; the initial slice is t = 0 [printed page 1, introduction to (1.1); printed page 5, (1.11)].",
      "q is the unknown state depending on x and t; the introductory system permits vector states in ℝ^m, with the scalar case m = 1 also discussed [printed page 1, paragraph following (1.1) and scalar-case paragraph].",
      "q_l and q_r are the constant left and right initial states [printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, opening paragraph and (1.11)].",
      "The initial jump is at a spatial point represented as x = 0; “say x = 0” presents the location as a choice [printed page 5, sentence introducing (1.11)]."
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The governing equation is hyperbolic [printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, “the hyperbolic equation together with special initial data”].",
      "The initial data are piecewise constant with a single jump discontinuity, represented at x = 0 [printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, sentence preceding (1.11)]."
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "A hyperbolic equation together with the specified two-state initial data is the problem called a Riemann problem [printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, opening paragraph].",
      "The initial condition requires q(x,0) = q_l for every x < 0 and q(x,0) = q_r for every x > 0; (1.11) imposes no displayed value at x = 0 [printed page 5, (1.11), two strict-inequality branches]."
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "The equation is part of the problem being defined; the two-state initial profile alone does not exhaust the definition [printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, “equation together with special initial data”].",
      "For the constant-coefficient linear system (1.1), hyperbolicity means real eigenvalues and a corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors of A [printed page 3, opening paragraph].",
      "The symbols q_l and q_r designate left and right states by the conditions x < 0 and x > 0, respectively [printed page 5, (1.11)]."
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The chapter introduces time-dependent problems and one-space-dimensional systems; (1.11) uses that spatial and temporal notation [printed page 1, paragraph introducing (1.1); printed page 5, (1.11)].",
      "Discontinuous conservation-law solutions need not satisfy the differential equation classically at the discontinuity; the source instead identifies the integral conservation law as fundamental there [printed page 5, paragraph beginning “At a discontinuity in q”].",
      "Conservation laws are an important class in the chapter, but the Riemann-problem definition says “hyperbolic equation”; the later discussion explicitly includes equations outside conservation form [printed page 3, Section 1.1; printed page 5, Section 1.2.1; printed page 8, second paragraph]."
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Quantifier scope and the interface",
